'''''Rosa villosa''''', the '''apple rose''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=ROVI80|taxon=Rosa villosa|accessdate=23 October 2015}}</ref> is a species of [[rose]]. It was described in 1753.<ref name="WiersemaLeón2013">{{cite book|author1=John H. Wiersema|author2=Blanca León|title=World Economic Plants: A Standard Reference, Second Edition|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=AIrNBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA1122|date=20 March 2013|publisher=CRC Press|isbn=978-1-4665-7681-0|page=1122}}</ref>

==Description==
Its fruit [[persistence (botany)|persists]] for an average of 30 days, and bears an average of 23 seeds per fruit. Fruits average 68.1% water, and their [[dry matter|dry weight]] includes 20.8% [[carbohydrate]]s and 0.8% [[lipid]]s.{{sfn|Ehrlén|Eriksson|1991}}

==Distribution==
It is native to Europe, where it is found in the central, southern and southeastern parts of the continent, including southwestern Russia.<ref name="AFE">{{Cite book|last1 = Kurtto| first1 = Arto| last2 = Lampinen| first2 = Raino| last3 = Junikka| first3 = Leo| date = 2004| title = Atlas florae Europaeae, distribution of vascular plants in Europe. 13: Rosaceae (Spiraea to Fragaria, excl. Rubus)| publisher = Committee for mapping the flora of Europe and Societas Biologica Fennica| location = Helsinki| isbn = 978-951-9108-14-8|pages = 98–102}}<!-- p. 100--></ref>

==Taxonomy==
It is most closely related to ''[[Rosa mollis]]'', with which it is sometimes confused.<!--pp. 98, 102}}--><ref name="AFE"/>

